WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.450
Senior Researcher Dr. Victor Stafford had
an obsession.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.950
He was one of the SCP Foundation’s most
brilliant minds, but right now, that mind

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:12.040
was being twisted by grief and confusion.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.320
One of his closest friends and confidantes
at the Foundation, an anomalous astrophysics

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.480
expert named Dr. Lilibeth Orion, had gone
missing.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.789
Not killed, not kidnapped.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:25.239
Truly disappeared without a trace.

00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:30.599
Back when Dr. Orion first fell off the map,
Dr. Stafford had led the charge into the emergency

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:31.599
investigation.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:35.850
It should come as a surprise to nobody that
employee turnaround at the SCP Foundation

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.200
is higher than perhaps any other organization
on Earth.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.670
We’ve made whole videos about the various
ways unfortunate Foundation operatives might

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.410
die in the line of duty, but few things are
worse than never having the closure of knowing

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.680
what actually happened to your missing friend.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.260
Dr. Orion had experienced a tragedy almost
a year before her mysterious disappearance.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.820
Her husband and young daughter were both killed
in a car accident.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.200
Naturally, rumors spread that perhaps the
grief had just gotten too much for the poor

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.730
doctor, but Dr. Stafford wouldn’t hear a
word of it.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:14.460
Part of him knew that Lilibeth Orion was still
alive, out there, somewhere.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.270
But he needed to discover exactly what had
happened to her.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.900
This is what sparked the obsession that would
destroy his respectable Foundation career,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.950
and perhaps even his mind.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:30.229
He’d stepped beyond professional boundaries
by accessing a number of deeply personal documents,

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:34.070
including personnel files and Site-19 project
histories.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:39.150
He read through reams of archival documentation
concerning unexplained disappearances of Foundation

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:44.870
personnel - including the disappearance of
05-7, several decades prior.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:50.530
Dr. Stafford’s search went on for twenty
years, still finding very little, except an

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:55.720
arcane pattern of connection between each
of those who disappeared: All were Foundation

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:01.110
employees with three degrees of separation
from one another, to put it very reductively.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.850
A huge number of disappearances, Dr. Lilibeth
Orion included.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.950
He didn’t know what was taking them, or
why, but he would bet his life on the belief

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.260
there was one force behind it all.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:19.130
And while Dr. Stafford had no real way of
knowing, he was indeed right.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:23.970
Nearly twenty years prior, Dr. Orion found
herself in a strange and unfamiliar place

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:24.990
upon waking.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:29.630
A year before that, she’d lived in a two-story
suburban home, but after the deaths of her

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:33.480
husband and child, she’d moved into an apartment
in the city.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.890
That house just had too many empty spaces
in it for her.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.380
But now she was neither in her old home nor
her new apartment: She was in a rustic, two-level

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.790
cottage, somewhere out in the countryside.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:49.540
Naturally, Dr. Orion first tried calling for
help, but there was no cell phone signal and

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.980
no wifi.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.800
After that, she tried fleeing the property
on foot, only to encounter its first anomalous

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.450
effect: Upon walking out of one edge of the
property, you simply reappear, entering the

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:02.450
other.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.650
The house sat at the center of a half-mile
of grassy land, cordoned off with a wholesome

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:07.650
picket fence.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.010
A lonely swing set sat near a tree in the
yard.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.320
No matter how many times Dr. Orion tried to
run past the confines of the building’s

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.470
land, she would appear facing the rustic little
cottage once more.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.599
Next, she started yelling and calling for
help.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:23.690
Nothing.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.769
Only birds and squirrels seemed to register
her cries, and they ran away in fear.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:31.720
Nothing about the cottage suggested its location.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.489
Was she even still in the United States anymore?

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:39.410
And the flat, distant, and uninhabited planes
stretching out beyond her only served to confirm

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:45.080
exactly what she feared: There would be nobody
out there to help her.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:49.930
This would be a terrifying situation for anyone,
but thankfully, Dr. Orion’s Foundation training

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.330
allowed her to keep her cool under pressure.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:56.390
She immediately switched into research and
investigation mode; perhaps there would be

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.870
some key to her escape or rescue elsewhere
in the cottage.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.450
Knowledge is power, and Dr. Orion certainly
didn’t like feeling powerless.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:10.220
The first and second floor of the cottage
felt relatively conventional: Furniture, knickknacks,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.800
charming - but slightly peeled - wallpaper.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.010
Nothing about it was overtly anomalous.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.720
Soon, however, she detected a strange noise
coming from the attic.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.709
Like the slow chugging of an old machine.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.680
Upon climbing up there, she found a strange
contraption made from wood and metal sitting

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.020
in the corner of the attic, with a plaque
affixed to the front.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.410
It also had several PVC pipes running out
of it and feeding into the wall, off into

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:34.990
an unknown location.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:37.700
Dr. Orion approached and studied the plaque.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.580
It read: “Please fill this machine once
per day at noontime.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.410
We cannot arrive with as much haste if the
machine runs dry.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:49.060
We hope you understand, and trust that you
will keep your side of the arrangement now

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.370
that we have kept ours.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.630
Thank you.”

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:52.889
How strange.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:55.100
She needed to investigate further.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.820
Down in the basement, she found another more
anomalous element to the house: A large clump

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.330
of strange, unidentifiable vegetation.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.470
It was largely a kind of murky green, with
notes of red and purple that made it seem

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.410
almost otherworldly.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:13.640
Strangest of all, when in the plant’s proximity,
she heard an odd, static-like noise coming

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:14.640
off of it.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.690
It also intermittently let off a strange,
red pulse.

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:23.190
It didn’t take long for Dr. Orion to intuit
that this pulse was actually morse code, delivering

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.620
two distinct messages on a loop.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.760
The first was “Thanks for keeping the plants
fed.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.139
Hit a snag in traffic, might take a bit longer
than expected.”, and the second was “Please

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:36.960
keep this hatch open only in good weather.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.479
Please keep it closed for rain, snow, and
the chill of winter.

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:41.479
Thank you.”

00:05:41.479 --> 00:05:46.350
Just as the message suggested, there was a
hatch in the ceiling up above, angled perfectly

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:51.330
to allow daylight to filter through onto the
alien vegetation whenever it was left open.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:56.620
The last notable thing that Dr. Orion found
in the house was seemingly the least supernatural

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:02.259
of the three, and yet, the most anomalous
- in the traditional sense of the word.

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:07.669
It was a closed-network SCP Foundation computer
terminal and a rather old model at that.

00:06:07.669 --> 00:06:11.940
She managed to boot it up and gain access
with her login credentials, but what she saw

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.759
definitely didn’t bring her any comfort.

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:20.610
She was currently trapped in the middle of
what the victims of this place call SCP-2508.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.730
The first and most prescient piece of news
was that this little predicament Dr. Orion

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.979
found herself in was a life sentence.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:32.289
Only one person would ever be trapped here
at a time, and they would remain here until

00:06:32.289 --> 00:06:35.430
they died, one way or another.

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:39.750
All she could do in the meantime is work to
maintain the house, and hope that whoever

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:43.740
its owner was would someday return and give
her some respite.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.979
That’s when she found a final note left
by O5-7.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:48.210
It read…

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.400
“I was driving home when it happened.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.220
It was about a 30 minute commute through a
lot of back roads, and I was terribly drunk.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.190
It was one of those "XK Class Scenario averted,
let's celebrate" occasions, so I didn't really

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.539
hesitate to down more than my fair share of
wine.

00:07:01.539 --> 00:07:06.100
When I arrived at my front porch, car askew
so that the front tires dug into the dirt

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.170
on my front lawn, I stumbled for the door.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:12.900
After a great amount of fidgeting I managed
to get my key in the lock, and finally got

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:13.900
the door open.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.590
But then when I looked around, it wasn't my
house at all.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:18.470
It was this place.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.520
Dumbfounded, I went back outside.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.720
Surely, I thought, I had accidentally entered
another house in my drunken stupor.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.500
But now not even the outside was as expected.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.480
The suburban-urban environment had shifted
to a countryside.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:35.780
My car was still there, sitting on the asphalt
road which was now a dirt road.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.580
Everything was different, and it was never
quite the same.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.520
That was all 23 years ago.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:46.930
No doubt my position as O5 was replaced long,
long ago, and here I am, having found startlingly

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:48.550
few answers.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:52.539
Every once in a while I'll find an old shoe
or a photograph of someone who came before

00:07:52.539 --> 00:07:57.830
me, and I am reminded how we are all chained
together in an oblivious and obligatory service

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:59.120
to this place.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.900
Lately the clouds keep rolling in, and the
plants in the basement are buzzing more than

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:03.900
usual.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.789
I hear the gurgling of water draining through
the pipes in the walls from that old dusty

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:10.850
machine, and sometimes I try to find where
that water goes.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:15.319
I think it goes out beyond the house, into
the grassy field in front of the swing set.

00:08:15.319 --> 00:08:20.289
When I lay on the grass, sometimes, if I listen
very closely and everything else is quiet,

00:08:20.289 --> 00:08:24.160
I swear I can hear noises coming from far
below the ground.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:28.720
They sound like some kind of clockwork mechanism,
its gears quietly humming.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:32.019
I don't know what it means, and I don't think
I ever will.

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:36.279
Undoubtedly, the most puzzling thing about
this place is really quite a small feature.

00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:39.579
There's a computer terminal in the study,
and it looks like it's one of the oldest things

00:08:39.579 --> 00:08:40.579
in the house.

00:08:40.579 --> 00:08:43.169
It's clearly from the Foundation, but it's…

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:45.470
Unsettling, to say the least.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.149
We would have known about this if the Foundation
knew, I'm sure.

00:08:48.149 --> 00:08:52.680
But if that's truly the case, then it still
doesn't explain how the anomaly got its number

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:53.680
designation.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.019
Is it arbitrary, or is it actually cataloged
in the outside world?

00:08:57.019 --> 00:09:00.089
I don't think it's possible to know from here.

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:04.410
Sometimes I feel like I'm waiting for something,
or for someone.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:09.230
It's not for someone to save me, but it's
as if I'm waiting to meet someone here.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:13.480
I dream of what that meeting might be, or
why it might be in the first place.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.800
But somewhere deep down I know that I will
almost certainly die here, and this long wait

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:23.670
will be passed down to another, to continue
waiting with bated breath for something, though

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:26.720
what exactly that something is we can't be
sure of.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.329
Maybe someday I'll learn how the food gets
restocked.”

00:09:29.329 --> 00:09:34.429
As she finished the note, Dr. Orion felt a
cold wave of dread wash over her.

00:09:34.429 --> 00:09:40.360
She wasn’t just trapped: She was the latest
in a long line of trapped, doomed people who’d

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.929
been confined and then perished in this lonely
little cottage, keeping the place in shape

00:09:44.929 --> 00:09:48.399
while someone or something made its long journey
back.

00:09:48.399 --> 00:09:53.040
If this was anything to go by, chances are,
she wouldn’t be the last one to perish here

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.949
after decades of waiting.

00:09:54.949 --> 00:10:01.170
As the months turned into years, and the years
turned into decades, Dr. Orion stuck it out.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:05.550
She learned some things about the place, things
she assumed she might be able to pass on to

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:06.550
others.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:10.360
Like the machine in the attic, the one that
needs water every day, is somehow tied to

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.389
both the integrity of the cottage itself and
its occupant.

00:10:13.389 --> 00:10:18.170
If not properly watered, the occupant will
begin to grow sick, and eventually, start

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.170
to die.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.720
Back on the outside, Dr. Victor Stafford was
finally disciplined for his extra-curricular

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:24.720
investigations.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.940
He was stripped of all his titles and clearances
for spending all his time searching into the

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.149
mystery of Dr. Lilibeth Orion.

00:10:30.149 --> 00:10:34.879
The Foundation frankly had more important
things to deal with.

00:10:34.879 --> 00:10:37.240
They thought Stafford was crazy.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:42.459
They had no idea how deep this mystery went,
about how many Foundation operatives were

00:10:42.459 --> 00:10:48.350
taken, or most important of all: The fact
that Stafford knew who would be taken next.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:52.490
Back in SCP-2508, things were different for
Dr. Orion.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:56.880
Nearly twenty years had passed, and she was
in a different stage of her life.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.899
To some extent, she’d come to terms with
her new reality.

00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:03.559
When she dreamed of her old life, she would
often wonder whether those dreams were her

00:11:03.559 --> 00:11:08.660
actual life, and her time in the cottage was
just a dark, recurring nightmare.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:12.339
Maybe she’d die here someday, and let another
take her place.

00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:13.870
Or maybe...She wouldn’t.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:17.869
Dr. Orion, you see, was always the curious
type.

00:11:17.869 --> 00:11:21.790
Even if she did die here, she’d never let
herself die without knowing what was really

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:24.059
happening at that strange little cottage.

00:11:24.059 --> 00:11:28.009
And even more importantly: What was happening
under it...

00:11:28.009 --> 00:11:33.029
Now go check out “The Horror of Body Stealing
Rain - SCP-3300” and “You'll NEVER Make

00:11:33.029 --> 00:11:38.500
It To SCP-084 - Static Tower” for more frightening
anomalous places you’ll never escape!

